  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 401 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\n**** Blustery Conditions Today ****\n\n09z/3am satellite imagery shows partial clearing across central\nIllinois: however, ample cloud cover is noted upstream across\nWisconsin/Minnesota thanks to a short-wave trough dropping\nthrough the Upper Midwest. Clouds will increase as this feature\nnears...and enough synoptic lift will be generated to warrant\nslight chance PoPs (20%) for light showers/sprinkles. Given \nmostly cloudy skies and strong CAA courtesy of NW winds gusting \n25-30mph, temperatures will struggle to rise today. Afternoon\nhighs will range from the lower 40s far north around Galesburg\nand Lacon to the lower 50s south of I-70. Meanwhile the wind\nwill make it feel like its in the 30s.\n\n\n**** First Snow Flakes of the Season ****\n\nOnce the short-wave trough drops into the Ohio River Valley,\nsynoptic subsidence on its backside will lead to partial\nclearing and dry conditions across much of the KILX CWA tonight.\nThe exception will be locations along/northeast of a Monticello\nto Paris line where very light lake-enhanced precip may arrive.\nWith a fetch of very cold air characterized by 850mb\ntemperatures of -6 to -7C flowing over the warm waters of \nsouthern Lake Michigan that are currently around 55F (13C), the\nstage will be set for a persistent band of lake-effect \nprecipitation. 00z and 06z Nov 12 CAMs have shifted the band \nfurther west than previous runs...with the HRRR and RRFS \nfocusing the most persistent banding along the Illinois/Indiana \nline from near Gary, Indiana southward to near Watseka. Forecast\nsoundings are initially warm enough to support rain showers \nduring the evening, but cool sufficiently for a change over to \nsnow showers late tonight into Friday morning. Some of this very\nlight rain/snow may drop as far south as a Monticello to Paris \nline overnight. As another subtle short-wave drops southward \nthrough the Great Lakes, the fetch over the lake will become a \nbit more N/NE for a time Friday morning. This will likely shift \nthe band even further west, with some solutions suggesting snow \nflakes as far southwest as a Bloomington...to Decatur...to \nMattoon line. With just very light snow expected and ground \ntemperatures still warm, only trace amounts of snow accumulation\non grassy surfaces are possible across the far NE KILX CWA. \nFurther north where the snow will be more persistent, \naccumulations become more likely...with the 00z REFS indicating \na 10-15% chance for greater than 0.5\" along/north of a Rantoul \nto Hoopeston line.\n\n**** Cold Weekend Then Warmer Next Week ****\n\nThe lake-effect band will shift NE and gradually dissipate late\nFriday as high pressure builds into the region from the west.\nGiven clearing skies and winds becoming nearly calm, a chilly\nweekend is in store with daytime highs in the 40s and overnight\nlows in the 20s. After that, the deep upper trough east of the\nMississippi River will retreat into eastern Canada and heights\nwill climb markedly next week. High temperatures will moderate\nto near seasonal values in the lower to middle 50s on \nMonday/Tuesday...then will climb back above normal into the 60s \nby the middle and end of next week.",
